.TH VSPRITE 2
.SH NAME
vsprite: parse, read, bounds, pixels, raster, canvas \- TempleOS vector sprites
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "draw.m";
include "math/vsprite.m";
vsprite := load Vsprite Vsprite->PATH;
Sprite, Elem: import vsprite;

vsprite->init();

(s, err) := vsprite->read(path);
(s, err) = vsprite->parse(text);

(r, ok) := s.bounds();
(px, r) = s.pixels(display);
(img, mask, r) = s.raster(display);

px = vsprite->canvas(elems, w, h, ox, oy, display);

e := vsprite->mkelem(kind, nargs);
e = vsprite->copyelem(e);
line := vsprite->elemline(e);
(e, err) = vsprite->parseline(line);
(elems, n, err) = vsprite->parselist(body);

pts := vsprite->polyptabs(e);
elems = vsprite->transform(elems, deg, scale);
t := s.transformed(deg, scale);

c := vsprite->nearest(rgba);
name := vsprite->kindname(kind);
kind := vsprite->kindof(name);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
A TempleOS sprite is an ordered queue of drawing elements \(em a display
list \(em not a raster.
.I Vsprite
is that list as a type: it reads one, says what rectangle it occupies,
and draws it.
.PP
The element vocabulary and its numbering are TempleOS's
.BR SPT_* ,
and the text form is what
.B scripts/tos_extract_sprites.py
writes into
.BR /lib/temple/sprites/*.spr :
a line per element, its name and then its arguments.
.B kindname
and
.B kindof
convert between the name and the number.
.PP
.B read
parses a
.B .spr
file and
.B parse
a string; both return a
.B Sprite
or an error.
.PP
.B bounds
returns the rectangle the sprite occupies, in the sprite's own
coordinates, and 0 if the list draws nothing.
It is computed from the elements' nominal extents with the pen's
half-width as a margin \(em the rule the extractor used to set the rect
of every
.B .bit
in
.BR /icons/temple ,
so a sprite rendered here and the
.B .bit
beside it are the same rectangle.
.PP
.B pixels
returns one palette index a pixel over that rectangle, row-major, with
.B TRANSP
where nothing drew.
It touches
.IR draw (2)
only for the text elements, which need a font, so a sprite of geometry
can be measured and rasterised with no display at all.
.PP
.B raster
is the same thing as an
.B RGB24
image and a
.B GREY1
mask, ready to
.IR draw (2).
.PP
.B canvas
is what
.B pixels
is built on, and takes the canvas rather than deriving it: an element
list, a width and height, and where the sprite's origin sits in it.
It exists because an editor draws the list onto a fixed page instead of
onto the sprite's box, and that difference in canvas should be the only
thing that makes an editor's render and a port's differ.
.IR temple / spriteedit (1)
uses it, and
.IR vspritetest (1)
measures the difference: the same list on a 680x530 page, cropped back to
the box, is identical for all 57 sprites it compares, three of which run
off the page's edge.
.PP
.BR mkelem ,
.BR copyelem ,
.BR elemline ,
.B parseline
and
.B parselist
are the element type and the text grammar, exported for the same reason:
an editor that carried its own copy could drift from this one.
.B nearest
says which of the sixteen a rendered colour is.
.PP
.B transform
turns an element list by
.I deg
degrees about the sprite's origin and scales it by
.IR scale ,
and
.B transformed
is the same thing on a whole
.BR Sprite ;
.B transformelem
does one element, which is what an editor transforming a selection wants.
The source list is not touched.
This is TempleOS's
.B SpriteTransformQue
and it is exact for every 2D element: a circle keeps its radius scaled, an
ellipse and a regular polygon keep their rotation, a
.B polypt
is re-walked as a fresh path of compass steps, and a
.B rect
is promoted to a rotated
.B rect
turned by the same angle.
.B polyptabs
is that re-walk's other half, the absolute points of a
.BR polypt ;
.B walkappend
extends one toward a point.
.PP
The
.B tag
field of an
.B Elem
is not used here.
An editor needs somewhere to mark an element selected, and a field costs
less than keeping a parallel array in step with every insertion.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/math/vsprite.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR grpalette (2),
.IR vspritetest (1),
.IR temple (1)
.SH BUGS
The
.B mesh
and
.B shiftmesh
elements are inert.
They carry the 3D meshes, which the ports load separately from
.BR /icons/temple/*.mesh ,
and drawing one here would need a projection this type has no business
owning.
.PP
A
.B rect
does not survive a transform as a
.BR rect .
TempleOS promotes it to a rotated
.BR rect ,
and a
.B rect
is filled where a rotated one is an outline, so a transformed rect is a
different picture.
That is the reference behaviour \(em
.B scripts/tos_extract_sprites.py
draws the two the same way \(em not this type's invention, but it means
.IR vspritetest (1)
cannot check a transform on the eleven sprites that contain one.
.PP
A
.B bitmap
element is moved by a transform, not resampled, and a transform rounds
half away from zero where the rasteriser rounds half to even.
The rasteriser's rule is set by the
.B .bit
files it has to match; nothing constrains the transform's, so it is the
one
.IR temple / spriteedit (1)
has always used.
.PP
An element list drawn on a canvas other than the sprite's own box is not
guaranteed to give the same pixels.
A flood fill that escapes the sprite runs to the canvas's edge, so a
bigger canvas can let it come back in somewhere the box would have
stopped it; a dither's checkerboard is in canvas coordinates; and the
rounding, though it is done on the element coordinate, recovers that
coordinate by subtracting an offset from a number a line interpolated at
canvas magnitudes, so a point landing within an ulp of a half can round
either way.
Neither happens for any of the 57 sprites
.IR vspritetest (1)
compares both ways, but the dither one is not hypothetical:
.B spriteplot_1
is 684 pixels different between its box and
.IR temple / spriteedit (1)'s
page, entirely checkerboard phase, and moving the page's origin one pixel
makes it identical.
It is not among the 57 because it contains text.
.PP
An inverted flood fill
.RB ( floodfillnot )
stops where a plain one would not, and no
.B .bit
can check it: the extractor takes the invert flag and never uses it, so
.IR vspritetest (1)
skips the one sprite in the tree that has one.
Until recently it did not stop at all \(em the fill compared the pixel
under it, packed positive, with the ink taken from the sign-extended
palette, so for any colour with red at or above 128 the two were never
equal and the fill ran to the canvas's edge.
.PP
The text elements go through
.IR draw (2)'s
font rather than TempleOS's 8x8 table, so they will not agree with a
.B .bit
the extractor rasterised through the table.
No sprite containing one is compared by
.IR vspritetest (1).
